course: dictionary
lesson: 4358
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word gum can mean different things, so context decides which meaning you need. Look at the words around it. «Teeth are brushed gently in small circles with a soft brush, because hard scrubbing hurts the gums» tells you gums are the pink flesh in your mouth, because you brush teeth and gums, not sweets.
explain: Other clues point the other way. Jake says, «Swallow your gum and it sits in your tummy», so this gum is chewing gum, the soft sweet you chew but do not swallow. «Koalas sleep about 18 to 22 hours a day, because their gum leaves give little energy» means gum here is a tree, a eucalyptus, because it has leaves.
explain: Always ask: what word would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense. If you put chewing gum into the koala sentence, it does not make sense, so you have picked the wrong meaning.
fact: Gum has three main meanings: chewing gum, the pink flesh around your teeth, and a sticky substance from some trees.
fact: Your gums are the pink flesh in your mouth that holds your teeth.
fact: Chewing gum is a soft sweet you chew for a long time but do not swallow.
fact: In Australia, a gum tree is a eucalyptus tree, and koalas eat its leaves.
fact: Red or bleeding gums can mean you need to brush better and see the dentist.
exercise: «Teeth are brushed gently in small circles with a soft brush, because hard scrubbing hurts the gums and wears the teeth.» What are the gums here? | *The pink flesh in your mouth that holds your teeth | A sticky substance from a tree | A tree in Australia | A kind of chewing sweet
exercise: Jake leans in and says, «Swallow your gum and it sits in your tummy.» What kind of gum is he talking about? | Gum from a gum tree | The gums in your mouth | *Chewing gum | Glue for paper
exercise: «Koalas sleep about 18 to 22 hours a day, because their gum leaves give little energy.» What does gum mean in this sentence? | The pink flesh in your mouth | A sticky glue | *A eucalyptus tree | A chewing gum sweet
test: Which meaning fits best in «Vitamin C helps wounds heal and keeps gums and skin healthy»? | Glue for paper | Chewing gum you blow bubbles with | *The pink flesh in your mouth that holds your teeth | A eucalyptus tree
test: What does gum mean in «She stuck the picture on the card with gum»? | *A sticky glue | The pink flesh in your mouth | A tree with long leaves | Chewing gum you can blow bubbles with
exam: Ella thinks gum in «Teeth are brushed gently... because hard scrubbing hurts the gums» means chewing gum. What is the best reason she is wrong? | Gums are only found on trees | Hard scrubbing is always good for chewing gum | *You cannot brush chewing gum, and the gums here are in the mouth | Brushing is only for sweets
